{"title":"2026 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 16","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the diagram, the right-angled isosceles triangle has a hypotenuse length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/2/ac25611a80251a53688f2883bdb0288ab5e707f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{8} \\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>An image is supposed to go here.  You can help us out by creating one and <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit\">editing it in</a></span>.  Thanks.</i>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>What is the smallest number of these triangles needed to completely cover a square with side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/e/90e5a8a73cda70a0edb9da30d1eb81198dd5c551.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 \\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/274ddc22761df25d433679449e6dfa2da370c3d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 4 \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 8 \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 16 \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 32 \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 64$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"378\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can use the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pythagorean_theorem\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Pythagorean theorem\">Pythagorean theorem</a> to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/0/f708f237972e8e7ea3281a525e7d5a66935f43d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2} + x^{2} = (\\sqrt{8})^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"130\" height=\"24\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/f/8ffd0baef3f7ce9fd3cc6c638a1dfcd90700aeeb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x^{2} = 8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"62\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/5/1650189e464925348cb4a9e021d66a74b4b8dd81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2} = \\frac{8}{2} = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"90\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/f/5df58902e1ea17396072b7d9966336175ee566db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\sqrt{4} = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"94\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of these isosceles right triangles must form a square with side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/3/ca323f6b69ea131a9a8f962f8ddbd8ddc59583d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/1/541f4f0ac5c46d4e572ab6d69e704f8ad1810583.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 \\times 4 = 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"82\" height=\"13\" /> of these squares are needed to fill an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/e/90e5a8a73cda70a0edb9da30d1eb81198dd5c551.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 \\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/e/90e5a8a73cda70a0edb9da30d1eb81198dd5c551.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 \\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> square, meaning <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/f/94fd21f38c920539669af6aed280c6ec347dcdba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 16 = \\boxed{\\textbf {(D) } 32}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"141\" height=\"30\" /> triangles are needed.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/7/ca7b4acb8eeb90e37fa1434cb61472589e78a050.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" /> is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/d/71df1b44d6caa4b0f798d48ff8ee72da386e4cb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />. Since the legs are equal, they must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/8/9688e581d9cf8f2ac6b9d775b8e970f188e282f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"26\" height=\"41\" /> of the hypotenuse, which would then be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/a/97ada948c00e624edb9cbe5a5d19c33b7ff03bfa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt{2} \\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"86\" height=\"43\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/3/ca323f6b69ea131a9a8f962f8ddbd8ddc59583d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />. Like in solution 1, this leads to the conclusion that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/0/e2003349a5cef6de96cf060bde9322a684905d5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf {(D) } 32}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"67\" height=\"30\" /> triangles are needed.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"2026 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">2026 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2026 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2026 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2026 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 15\">Problem 15</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2026 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 17\">Problem 17</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2026 CEMC Gauss (Grade 